Data was collected via a custom-built, automated data acquisition system integrated with the experimental apparatus. Participants (n=120, balanced for age and sex) completed a series of trials over three separate sessions, each spaced 72 hours apart to minimize carryover effects. Physiological data – specifically, EEG and fMRI – were recorded simultaneously during each trial. 

EEG data was acquired using a 64-channel Geodesic Sensor Net, sampled at 1000Hz, and pre-processed using EEGLAB with independent component analysis to remove artifact. fMRI data was obtained using a 3T Siemens Prisma scanner with a TR of 2 seconds. Pre-processing involved slice-timing correction, motion correction, spatial normalization to MNI space, and smoothing with an 8mm FWHM Gaussian kernel. 

